 # Isosurface extraction
 
-Also called: Marching Cubes family, contouring, polygonization
+Also called: contouring, polygonization, marching cubes family
 
 ## Overview
-Isosurface extraction converts a discretized scalar field into a polygonal mesh (typically triangles) representing the surface where the scalar value equals a specified isovalue [S1, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing implicit 3D structures by approximating the continuous zero-level set with a discrete manifold. Unlike [Blocky meshing](31-blocky-meshing.md), which produces voxel-aligned "blocky" geometry based on occupancy states, isosurface methods generate smooth, curved surfaces by interpolating between grid vertices [S1, S4, unofficial].
+Isosurface extraction converts a discretized scalar field into a polygonal mesh (typically triangles) representing the locus of points where the field equals a specific threshold (isovalue) [S1, S5, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing implicit 3D structures by transforming volumetric data into explicit surface geometry suitable for rendering or simulation [S6, unofficial]. Unlike [Blocky meshing](31-blocky-meshing.md), which produces voxel-aligned "cuberille" meshes, isosurface methods generate smooth, curved approximations by interpolating within cells [S1, unofficial][S12].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on a discretized domain, typically a regular grid (Cartesian, octree, or tetrahedral) where each cell contains scalar values or occupancy states at its vertices or centers. The core mechanism involves traversing the grid cells and evaluating the data to determine which parts of the domain constitute the surface.
+All methods in this family operate on a discretized domain, typically a regular grid (Cartesian, octree, or tetrahedral) where each cell contains scalar values or occupancy states at its vertices or centers. The core mechanism involves traversing the grid cells and evaluating the data to determine which parts of the domain constitute the surface [G].
 
