 ### [Streaming Marching Cubes](6190-streaming-marching-cubes.md)
-With Hash Table Vertex Sharing is a method for extracting isosurfaces from large volumetric datasets that combines memory-efficient streaming processing with explicit vertex sharing to ensure topological consistency. It solves the problem of generating watertight, shared-vertex meshes from massive data that cannot fit entirely in memory, while avoiding the vertex duplication common in naive streaming implementations. It differs from standard [Marching Cubes Lookup and Configuration](2518-marching-cubes-lookup-and-configuration.md) by explicitly managing vertex identity across cell boundaries via a hash table, and from [Sparse Marching Cubes](3825-sparse-marching-cubes.md) by focusing on the streaming memory constraint rather than spatial sparsity.
